Since the altitude to the hypotenuse of the 30-60-90 triangle divides the isosceles triangle into a 45-45 triangle and a 30-60 triangle with hypotenuse length $r$,
the base of the isosceles triangle for the right triangle is $\frac{r\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
So the hypotenuse for the right triangle is $\frac{r}{\cos15^\circ}=\frac{r}{\cos(45^\circ-30^\circ)}=\frac{r}{\cos45^\circ\cos30^\circ+\sin 45^\circ \sin30^\circ}
=\frac{2r}{\sqrt{6}+1}=2r(\sqrt{6}-1)$. So
from the Pythagorean Theorem, the height to the hypotenuse for the right triangle is $\sqrt{(2r(\sqrt{6}-1))^2-r^2}=\sqrt{r^2(4\sqrt{6}-4)}=r\sqrt{4\sqrt{6}-4}$.
From the lower right 30-60-90 triangle,
$r\sqrt{4\sqrt{6}-4}=3$.
Solving for $r$ gives $r = \sqrt[3]{\frac{12}{4-4\sqrt{6}}}$ .
The area of the isosceles right triangle is $\frac{3^2}{2}=\boxed{\frac{9}{2}}$, expressed in square units. </think> <answer> \frac{9}{2} </answer>

